(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-hemostasis-quiz-version-4"]={"title":"Hemostasis — Quiz 3 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Extramedullary hematopoiesis (EMH) refers to blood cell production occurring outside the bone marrow. In adults, pathologic EMH most often occurs in which organs?","choices":["The skin and muscle","The liver and spleen","The kidneys and lungs","The thymus and tonsils"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When adult hematopoietic stem cells are stimulated (e.g., by bone irradiation or certain anemias) to produce blood cells outside the marrow, this pathologic extramedullary hematopoiesis occurs mostly in the liver and spleen — the same organs that serve as the major hematopoietic sites earlier in fetal development. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 12"},{"q":"Across development, the major site of hematopoiesis shifts over time. During mid-gestation, blood cell production occurs mainly in the...","choices":["liver (plus spleen and lymph nodes)","the bone marrow cavity exclusively","the embryonic yolk sac exclusively","the fetal thymus gland exclusively"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. During the mid-trimester of gestation, hematopoiesis occurs mainly in the liver, along with contributions from the spleen and lymph nodes, before the bone marrow becomes the dominant site later in gestation and throughout postnatal life. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 7"},{"q":"As red blood cells age over their lifespan, their metabolic enzymes gradually deplete, which leads to...","choices":["a compensatory increase in hemoglobin synthesis intended to offset the ongoing enzymatic decline","increased cell rigidity that paradoxically improves oxygen delivery during the final weeks of life","no functional change of any kind until the moment the cell is finally replaced","increased cell fragility, causing rupture as cells squeeze through tight passages (often in the spleen)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Depletion of metabolic enzymes with age makes red blood cells progressively more fragile, so that many finally rupture while being forced through narrow passages, particularly within the spleen. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 27"},{"q":"When aged red blood cells finally rupture, the released hemoglobin is phagocytosed by macrophages located particularly in the...","choices":["kidney and bladder","liver and spleen","lungs and heart","skin and subcutaneous tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Macrophages in the liver and spleen are the primary sites where hemoglobin released from ruptured red blood cells is phagocytosed and broken down. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 28"},{"q":"Normal circulating platelet count falls within which approximate range?","choices":["1,000–5,000 per microliter of blood","4.5–6 million per microliter of blood","150,000–450,000 per microliter of blood","4,000–11,000 per microliter of blood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Platelets normally circulate at a concentration of roughly 150,000 to 450,000 per microliter of blood. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 31"},{"q":"Platelets have a circulating half-life of about 8–12 days, after which they are cleared mainly by...","choices":["renal filtration and excretion in urine","digestion in the gastrointestinal tract","tissue macrophages, mostly in the spleen","filtration within the pulmonary capillary bed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Platelets are eliminated from circulation mainly by tissue macrophages, particularly in the spleen — the same organ implicated in removing aged red blood cells. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 31"},{"q":"Consistent with the spleen's role in removing platelets from circulation, splenectomy characteristically results in...","choices":["a decrease in circulating platelet count","no change in platelet count, since the spleen has no role in platelet handling","an increase in circulating platelet count","a decrease in red blood cell count only, with platelets unaffected"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Because the spleen normally removes platelets from the blood, removing the spleen (splenectomy) reduces this clearance and increases the circulating platelet count. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 59"},{"q":"The thrombin–thrombomodulin complex, formed when thrombin binds thrombomodulin on intact endothelium, activates a plasma protein called Protein C, which then acts to limit clotting by...","choices":["directly converting fibrinogen into fibrin","inactivating activated Factors Va and VIIIa","directly activating Factor XII to start the intrinsic pathway","stimulating additional platelet aggregation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Once activated by the thrombin–thrombomodulin complex, Protein C inactivates activated Factors Va and VIIIa, removing two key cofactors needed for efficient prothrombin and Factor X activation, thereby restraining further clot growth. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 48"},{"q":"Once a fibrin clot forms, fibrin fibers themselves help limit further clot growth by binding roughly what fraction of the thrombin present, localizing it within the clot?","choices":["85–90%","Essentially 0%, since fibrin does not bind thrombin","100%, removing all thrombin from the circulation instantly","About 10%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fibrin fibers bind an estimated 85–90% of the thrombin generated, sequestering it within the clot itself; this localizes thrombin's action and helps prevent it from diffusing into the general circulation and triggering widespread clotting. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 49"},{"q":"Vitamin K's essential role in coagulation is to serve as a cofactor for carboxylation of glutamic acid residues in prothrombin and Factors VII, IX, and X, and also in...","choices":["Factor XIII (fibrin-stabilizing factor)","Fibrinogen (Factor I)","Tissue factor (Factor III)","Protein C"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vitamin K–dependent carboxylation is required not only for prothrombin and Factors VII, IX, and X but also for Protein C, one of the body's own natural anticoagulant proteins. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 54"},{"q":"During the carboxylation reaction it enables, vitamin K itself becomes oxidized and inactivated; it is then reactivated by...","choices":["circulating antithrombin III protein","vitamin K epoxide reductase complex 1 (VKORC1)","the fibrin-stabilizing Factor XIII enzyme","endothelial-bound thrombomodulin protein complex"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The enzyme VKORC1 reduces the oxidized, inactive form of vitamin K back to its active form, allowing it to be reused for further carboxylation reactions. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 54"},{"q":"Tissue plasminogen activator (tPA), which converts trapped plasminogen to plasmin over the days following clot formation, is a glycoprotein produced mainly by the...","choices":["red blood cells","liver hepatocytes","vascular endothelium","platelets exclusively"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Human tPA is a glycoprotein produced mainly by vascular endothelial cells; released over several days after injury, it converts plasminogen trapped within the clot into active plasmin. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 51"},{"q":"Plasmin, the active protease that digests fibrin fibers during clot lysis, structurally resembles which other enzyme discussed in digestion?","choices":["Pepsin","Trypsin","Lactase","Salivary amylase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Plasmin is described as a protease resembling trypsin; like trypsin, it is generated from an inactive precursor (plasminogen) and then cleaves target proteins — in plasmin's case, fibrin fibers and several other clotting factors. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 51"},{"q":"In the standard table of clotting factors and their synonyms, Factor I, Factor II, Factor III, and Factor IV correspond respectively to...","choices":["prothrombin, fibrinogen, calcium ions, and tissue factor","tissue factor, calcium ions, fibrinogen, and prothrombin","calcium ions, tissue factor, prothrombin, and fibrinogen","fibrinogen, prothrombin, tissue factor, and calcium ions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. By convention, Factor I is fibrinogen, Factor II is prothrombin, Factor III is tissue factor (thromboplastin), and Factor IV is calcium ions — the numbering system used throughout the classic clotting factor table. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 39"},{"q":"Fibrin-stabilizing factor (Factor XIII), which is found both in plasma and released from platelets, is activated by thrombin and then strengthens a clot by...","choices":["converting fibrinogen directly into fibrin monomers via thrombin cleavage","activating plasminogen into active plasmin enzyme","covalently cross-linking fibrin monomers and adjacent fibrin fibers","digesting fibrin fibers to begin the clot removal process"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Activated Factor XIII covalently cross-links fibrin monomers and adjacent fibrin fibers; without this cross-linking step, the resulting clot remains structurally weak. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 40"},{"q":"Vascular spasm after vessel injury results from three combined factors: local myogenic contraction, local autacoids released from tissue/endothelium/platelets, and...","choices":["parasympathetic reflexes originating from pain fibers that promote vasodilation","direct action of circulating erythropoietin on the vessel wall","nervous reflexes from pain fibers that activate sympathetic vasoconstriction","release of intrinsic factor from gastric parietal cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The third contributing factor to vascular spasm is a nervous reflex: sensory (pain) impulses from the injured vessel activate sympathetic nerves, adding neurally mediated vasoconstriction to the myogenic and autacoid-driven components. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 33"},{"q":"Myogenic contraction is described as the most important vasoconstrictive factor specifically for...","choices":["venules exclusively","capillaries only","large blood vessels","the pulmonary vasculature exclusively"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Direct myogenic contraction of the vessel wall in response to damage is identified as the most important factor for constricting large blood vessels, while other mechanisms (like thromboxane A2) are emphasized more for smaller vessels. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 33"},{"q":"Of the body's total iron (roughly 4–5 g), what fraction is found within hemoglobin?","choices":["About 10%","About 95%","About 65%","About 30%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Roughly 65% of total body iron is contained within hemoglobin, with much smaller fractions in myoglobin (about 4%), intracellular heme compounds (about 1%), and iron bound to circulating transferrin (about 0.1%). — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 24"},{"q":"Iron absorbed from the diet enters the plasma and combines with apotransferrin — a protein classified as a...","choices":["immunoglobulin (antibody)","fibrinogen","beta globulin","albumin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Apotransferrin is a beta globulin plasma protein; once it binds iron, it becomes transferrin, which carries the iron loosely bound so it can be released to any tissue cell that needs it. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 25"},{"q":"Excess absorbed iron not immediately needed is stored, combined with apoferritin to form ferritin, mainly within...","choices":["circulating plasma, loosely bound to transferrin","hepatocytes and reticuloendothelial cells of the bone marrow","skeletal muscle tissue and myoglobin exclusively","renal tubular epithelial cells exclusively"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Excess iron is stored as ferritin mainly in hepatocytes and in reticuloendothelial cells of the bone marrow, providing a reserve that can be mobilized when dietary iron intake is insufficient. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 25"},{"q":"When platelets contact exposed collagen at an injury site, they extend pseudopods, become sticky, and release granule contents including growth factors and clotting factors such as...","choices":["pepsinogen","intrinsic factor","thrombospondin","secretin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Activated platelets release granule contents that include growth factors and clotting factors such as thrombospondin, contributing to platelet aggregation and the broader hemostatic and repair response. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 35"},{"q":"Erythropoiesis proceeds at a remarkably high baseline rate in healthy adults, producing approximately how many new erythrocytes per second?","choices":["About 2.4 billion per second","About 2,400 per second","About 24 per second","About 2.4 million per second"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Human adults normally produce approximately 2.4 million new red blood cells every second, reflecting the enormous ongoing turnover needed to replace senescent cells and maintain red cell mass. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 9"},{"q":"Hematopoiesis adapts to changing physiological demands: hypoxia specifically drives increased...","choices":["platelet production exclusively, with no effect on red or white cell lines","WBC production, while infection/inflammation instead drives erythropoiesis","equal increases in all three cell lines regardless of the specific stimulus","erythropoiesis, while infection/inflammation instead drives increased WBC production"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hematopoietic output is demand-responsive: hypoxia is the stimulus that ramps up erythropoiesis (via erythropoietin), while infection/inflammation is the stimulus that instead ramps up white blood cell production — distinct triggers for distinct lineages. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 9"},{"q":"Somewhat counterintuitively, a clean, minimally traumatic cut (such as from a razor) tends to bleed more than a ragged, crushing injury, largely because...","choices":["ragged cuts expose more surface area for platelet bonding and cause more trauma to activate prothrombin activator","clean cuts release considerably more tissue factor into the wound than ragged cuts do","ragged, crushing cuts somehow always manage to avoid severing any blood vessels at all","clean, minimally traumatic cuts sever a greater number of blood vessels than ragged cuts do"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ragged, more traumatic wounds provide greater surface area for platelets to bond to and generate more tissue trauma, which more strongly activates prothrombin activator formation — so, paradoxically, they can bleed less than a clean razor cut, which offers less surface area and weaker activation of the clotting cascade. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 37"},{"q":"In the classic hemostasis timeline, visible fibrin typically appears within about 15–20 seconds after severe trauma, compared to roughly how long after only minor trauma?","choices":["About 2 seconds","About 2 minutes","About 24 hours","Fibrin never appears after minor trauma, only after severe trauma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With only minor trauma, fibrin formation takes longer to appear — about 2 minutes — compared to the much faster 15–20 seconds seen after severe trauma, reflecting the greater and more rapid activation of the clotting cascade with more severe injury. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 38"},{"q":"Prothrombin, the plasma protein cleaved by prothrombin activator to form thrombin, is classified as a(n)...","choices":["alpha2-globulin, synthesized in the liver in a vitamin K-dependent manner","an albumin variant, synthesized within the exocrine pancreas","an immunoglobulin, synthesized by antibody-producing plasma cells","a beta globulin, synthesized within the renal cortical tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Prothrombin is an alpha2-globulin (molecular weight ~68,700, about 15 mg/dL in plasma), synthesized by the liver through a vitamin K-dependent process — consistent with vitamin K's broader role in activating several clotting factors. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 40"},{"q":"Beyond the iron actively incorporated into hemoglobin, myoglobin, and enzymes, an additional 15–30% of total body iron is held in reserve, stored mainly as ferritin in the liver and...","choices":["skeletal muscle and myoglobin tissue","the reticuloendothelial system","the lung parenchyma tissue","the thyroid gland tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Roughly 15–30% of total body iron is stored as ferritin, mainly in the liver and the reticuloendothelial system, providing a mobilizable reserve beyond the iron actively in use within hemoglobin, myoglobin, and iron-containing enzymes. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 24"},{"q":"Endogenous heparin's anticoagulant action is significantly potentiated when it binds to...","choices":["fibrinogen","antithrombin III","von Willebrand factor","Factor XIII"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Heparin dramatically accelerates antithrombin III's ability to neutralize thrombin and other clotting factors; binding heparin markedly potentiates antithrombin III's inherent (but otherwise slow) inhibitory action. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 46"},{"q":"Platelet production from megakaryocytes is regulated by a hormone called...","choices":["erythropoietin","angiotensinogen","gastrin","thrombopoietin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thrombopoietin, produced mainly by the liver, stimulates megakaryocyte growth and maturation, driving the fragmentation process that releases new platelets into circulation. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 31"},{"q":"Von Willebrand factor facilitates platelet adhesion at an injury site by binding to proteins on both exposed subendothelial collagen and...","choices":["platelet surface receptors","hepatocyte membranes","red blood cell membranes exclusively","gastric parietal cell receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Von Willebrand factor acts as a molecular bridge, binding both exposed subendothelial collagen and specific receptors on the platelet surface, allowing platelets to adhere firmly to the site of vessel injury even under the shear forces of flowing blood. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, Slide 34"}]};
